C = [
("h1", "2. The Source"),
("p", "Claim: the first question to ask of a prophet’s word is where it came from. Jeremiah 23 and Ezekiel 13 answer it in the LORD’s own words: a word is his if the prophet stood in his counsel and was sent, and it is not his if it is the vision of the prophet’s own heart. Conduct is evidence of the source, but it is not the test."),
("sub", "2.1 The charge: not sent, not spoken to"),
("p", "Jeremiah 23 opens with the prophet’s own distress. “Mine heart within me is broken because of the prophets; all my bones shake” (Jeremiah 23:9). The LORD then names two things wrong with the prophets of Samaria and of Jerusalem. Of Samaria: “they prophesied in Baal, and caused my people Israel to err” (Jeremiah 23:13). Of Jerusalem: “they commit adultery, and walk in lies: they strengthen also the hands of evildoers, that none doth return from his wickedness” (Jeremiah 23:14). The first charge is what they prophesied by, the second is how they lived and what their living did to the people."),
("p", "Wessels, in a study of this passage, draws the conclusion that the rival prophets of Jeremiah 23:9–15 are known to be false by their conduct and because the LORD did not send them (Wessels, in the Journal for Semitics). The volume agrees as far as the text goes, and notes where the text carries the weight. Verse 14 puts the conduct plainly, and verse 21 gives the reason that the conduct is evidence of: “I have not sent these prophets, yet they ran: I have not spoken to them, yet they prophesied” (Jeremiah 23:21). Conduct points toward the source. The source itself is stated in verse 21."),
("sub", "2.2 Whose heart the vision comes from"),
("p", "The decisive sentence is in verse 16. “Thus saith the LORD of hosts, Hearken not unto the words of the prophets that prophesy unto you: they make you vain: they speak a vision of their own heart, and not out of the mouth of the LORD” (Jeremiah 23:16). The test is a question of origin. A vision is either out of the mouth of the LORD or out of the heart of the man. The people are told not to hearken to the second kind, and the instruction is given on that ground and on no other."),
("p", "The next verse shows what such a vision sounds like and who likes it. “They say still unto them that despise me, The LORD hath said, Ye shall have peace; and they say unto every one that walketh after the imagination of his own heart, No evil shall come upon you” (Jeremiah 23:17). The false word says what the hearer already wishes. It uses the LORD’s name, “The LORD hath said,” and it offers peace to the very people who despise him. This is the pattern the volume will meet again at Jeremiah 28 and in 1 Kings 22, and Diamond’s observation about Ahab, taken up in chapter 8, belongs here."),
("sub", "2.3 Who has stood in the counsel of the LORD"),
("p", "The LORD gives the positive mark in a question. “For who hath stood in the counsel of the LORD, and hath perceived and heard his word? who hath marked his word, and heard it?” (Jeremiah 23:18). Then, of the false prophets: “But if they had stood in my counsel, and had caused my people to hear my words, then they should have turned them from their evil way, and from the evil of their doings” (Jeremiah 23:22). The true prophet has been in the LORD’s counsel, and the effect of his word is to turn the people from evil. The false prophet has not been there, and the effect of his word is to strengthen the hands of evildoers (Jeremiah 23:14)."),
("p", "The LORD adds two warnings against passing off a dream as his word. “The prophet that hath a dream, let him tell a dream; and he that hath my word, let him speak my word faithfully. What is the chaff to the wheat?” (Jeremiah 23:28). A dream may be told as a dream. What is forbidden is giving it the standing of the LORD’s word. Then the force of the true word: “Is not my word like as a fire? saith the LORD; and like a hammer that breaketh the rock in pieces?” (Jeremiah 23:29). The question of source is therefore also a question of effect, but the text puts the source first."),
("p", "Prophetic Commentary Volume 2 reads Jeremiah 23:25 and 27 as part of its argument about the false prophet of Revelation 13. This volume does not take up that identification. It reads the same sentences for what they say of every prophet in Jeremiah’s day: those who say “I have dreamed, I have dreamed” in the LORD’s name are, in his words, “prophets of the deceit of their own heart” (Jeremiah 23:25, 26)."),
("sub", "2.4 Ezekiel 13: the same test, a generation on"),
("p", "Ezekiel is commanded to prophesy against “the prophets of Israel that prophesy, and say thou unto them that prophesy out of their own hearts, Hear ye the word of the LORD” (Ezekiel 13:2). The woe is on “the foolish prophets, that follow their own spirit, and have seen nothing” (Ezekiel 13:3). The test is the same as Jeremiah’s, and the wording is almost the same: their prophecy is from their own hearts, and the LORD has not sent them. “They have seen vanity and lying divination, saying, The LORD saith: and the LORD hath not sent them” (Ezekiel 13:6). Again: “Have ye not seen a vain vision, and have ye not spoken a lying divination, whereas ye say, The LORD saith it; albeit I have not spoken?” (Ezekiel 13:7)."),
("p", "Ezekiel adds the judgment on such prophets: “they shall not be in the assembly of my people, neither shall they be written in the writing of the house of Israel, neither shall they enter into the land of Israel” (Ezekiel 13:9). The prophets are cut off from the register of Israel and from the land. Study 68 reads Ezekiel 13:9 as one of the registers of names; this volume reads it as the LORD’s sentence on prophets who spoke from their own hearts. The two uses do not conflict."),
("p", "Between the two prophets, two generations apart, the same question is asked of the same kind of word. That fact matters for the volume’s thesis. The test of source is not peculiar to one prophet’s circumstances. It is the LORD’s own standard, applied by Jeremiah in Jerusalem before the exile and by Ezekiel among the exiles."),
("sub", "2.5 Why conduct is evidence and not the test"),
("p", "Conduct is real evidence. Jeremiah 23:14 names adultery and lies, and Jeremiah 23:10 and 11 name the profaneness of prophet and priest. But the volume holds that conduct is not the test, for two reasons drawn from the text. First, the LORD gives the test as origin: “not out of the mouth of the LORD” (Jeremiah 23:16). A man might live well and still speak from his own heart, and the passage does not say that good conduct validates a vision. Second, the LORD’s own complaint is stated as a fact about the sending: “I have not sent these prophets” (Jeremiah 23:21). Conduct tells the hearer something about the likelihood. The sending is the question."),
("ctbl", "The test of source in Jeremiah 23 and Ezekiel 13.", [
    ("Mark", "False prophet", "True prophet"),
    ("Sent?", "“I have not sent these prophets, yet they ran” (Jeremiah 23:21)", "Sent by the LORD (Jeremiah 26:12)"),
    ("Where from?", "“a vision of their own heart” (Jeremiah 23:16); “out of their own hearts” (Ezekiel 13:2)", "“stood in the counsel of the LORD” (Jeremiah 23:18)"),
    ("Effect on hearers", "Peace to those who despise him; strengthens evildoers (Jeremiah 23:14, 17)", "Turns them from their evil way (Jeremiah 23:22)"),
    ("The LORD’s verdict", "“I am against the prophets” (Jeremiah 23:30); not in the register of Israel (Ezekiel 13:9)", "“he that hath my word, let him speak my word faithfully” (Jeremiah 23:28)"),
  ], [0.2, 0.42, 0.38]),
("sub", "2.6 Micah: full of power by the spirit of the LORD"),
("p", "The charge of Jeremiah 23 is not Jeremiah’s alone. A century earlier Micah describes the same trade and gives its mark. The LORD speaks “concerning the prophets that make my people err, that bite with their teeth, and cry, Peace; and he that putteth not into their mouths, they even prepare war against him” (Micah 3:5). The prophets cry “Peace” to the man who feeds them and prepare war against the man who does not. Their word follows the table. That is as plain a description of a word that comes from the prophet’s own interest as the Scripture gives."),
("p", "The judgment is a silence. “Therefore night shall be unto you, that ye shall not have a vision; and it shall be dark unto you, that ye shall not divine” (Micah 3:6). The seers “shall all cover their lips; for there is no answer of God” (Micah 3:7). The last clause is the point. A prophet is a man to whom God answers, and when the answer stops, the trade has nothing left to sell. The false prophet’s craft was to go on speaking when the LORD had stopped."),
("p", "Micah then puts himself beside them, and what he says of himself is the positive side of the test of source. “But truly I am full of power by the spirit of the LORD, and of judgment, and of might, to declare unto Jacob his transgression, and to Israel his sin” (Micah 3:8). He does not offer a reputation, a school or a sign. He says the word he carries comes by the spirit of the LORD, and he names its content: the transgression of Jacob and the sin of Israel. The true prophet claims a source and takes the consequences of the claim."),
("p", "The volume argues from this that the source of a word shows itself in what the word does to its hearers. A word that exposes “his transgression” and “his sin” is a word that costs the one who carries it, and Micah’s peers prepared war against the man who would not feed them. The word that cries “Peace” is not wrong because it is gentle. It is wrong when the people’s sin is unspoken and the speaker is paid for the silence. Lamentations 2:14 gives the verdict in a single sentence: “Thy prophets have seen vain and foolish things for thee: and they have not discovered thine iniquity, to turn away thy captivity.” A prophecy that does not uncover iniquity cannot turn captivity, and the captivity came."),
("sub", "2.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The first question of any prophetic word is where it came from: out of the mouth of the LORD, or out of the prophet’s own heart.",
    "A word the prophet has stood in the LORD’s counsel to receive turns the hearers from evil; a word from his own heart comforts them in it.",
    "Conduct is evidence of the source and is not the test; the LORD states the test as sending and speaking.",
    "A dream may be told as a dream; it may not be given the standing of the LORD’s word.",
    "Jeremiah and Ezekiel, a generation apart, apply the same test in the same terms.",
    "Micah 3:5–8 and Lamentations 2:14 give the mark of the paid word: it cries “Peace” and leaves the iniquity unspoken; the true prophet is full of power by the spirit of the LORD to declare the transgression.",
  ]),
]
